.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt WHOAMI 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm whoami
.Nd print effective user name
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm whoami
.Op Fl hV
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility prints the user name associated with the current effective
user ID to the standard output.
It is functionally equivalent to:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
id -un
.Ed
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Display a help message and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Output version information and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std whoami
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print the current user name:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ whoami
alice
.Ed
.Pp
Use in a script to check if running as root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if [ "$(whoami)" = "root" ]; then
    echo "Running as root"
fi
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr id 1 ,
.Xr logname 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is not specified by POSIX but is widely available on
Unix-like systems.
The behavior is compatible with GNU coreutils.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
